			"id": "instance_page_state:page:f68luzkeSOzKLbQERhXxs",
			"pageId": "page:f68luzkeSOzKLbQERhXxs",
			"typeName": "instance_page_state"
		},
		{
			"x": -57.883668508716255,
			"y": 5831.952142716986,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:FZQyNSDw7SQOlNNPD5DIh",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1454.34375,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "So far we saw two types of servers master and worker, and a set of components that make up Kubernetes. But how are these components distributed across different types of servers?. In other words, how does one server become a master and the other the slave? The worker node or minion as it is also known, is where the containers are hosted. For example, Docker containers and to run docker containers on a system we need container runtime installed. That's where the container runtime falls. In this case it happens to be Docker. This doesn't have to be Docker. There are other container runtime alternatives available, such as Rocker or Cryo. But throughout this course we are going to use Docker as our container runtime engine. The master server has the Kube API server and that is what makes it a master. Similarly, the worker nodes have the Kubelet agent that is responsible for interacting with the master to provide health information of the worker node and carryout actions requested by the master on the worker nodes.  All these information gathered are stored in a key value store on the master. The key value store is based on the popular etcd framework. As we just discussed the master also has the control manager and the scheduler. There are other components as well. The reason we went through this is to understand what components constitue the master and worker nodes. This will help us install and configure the right components on different systems."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
			"parentId": "page:page",
			"index": "b1xcJf",
			"typeName": "shape"
		},
		{
			"x": -83.12020864084838,
			"y": 9411.135583052344,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:714hpzFY1oEAV6aoDKvu9",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "s",
				"w": 1639.8823438359166,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 1,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"attrs": {
						"dir": "auto"
					},
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "The recommended solution is nerdcontrol tool. The nerdcontrol tool is a command line tool that's very similar to Docker. So it's like Docker like CLI for containerd. It supports all or most of the features that docker supports. apart from that it has the added benefit that it can give us access to the newest features implemented into containerd."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
